          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            While a (left or right)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint"} adjoint
            to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            may be understood as the best approximation (from one side or the other) of a possibly non-existent
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse"} inverse
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functors"} adjoint functors
            restricts to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subcategories"} subcategories
            %em center
            of the adjunction, their objects are sometimes known as the
            %em fixed points
            of the adjunction.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalences+of+categories"} equivalences of categories
            that arise from fixed points of adjunctions this way are often known as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dualities"} dualities
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pontrjagin+duality"} Pontrjagin duality
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gelfand+duality"} Gelfand duality
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stone+duality"} Stone duality
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Isbell+duality"} Isbell duality
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+rings"} commutative rings
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+schemes"} affine schemes
            (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#PorstTholen91"} Porst-Tholen 91
          %h2#definition Definition
          #FixedPointEquivalenceOfAnAdjunction.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              %strong (fixed point equivalence of an adjunction)
            %p Let
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %munderover
                      %mrow
                        %mphantom
                          %mi AA
                        %mo ⊥
                        %mphantom
                          %mi AA
                      %munder
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mi R
                      %mover
                        %mo ⟵
                        %mi L
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{D}
                    \\underoverset
                    {\underset{ R }{\longrightarrow}}
                    {\overset{ L }{\longleftarrow}}
                    {\phantom{AA}\bot\phantom{AA}}
                    \\mathcal{C}
            %p
              be a pair of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functors"} adjoint functors
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  an
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi c
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi 𝒞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \in \mathcal{C}
                  is a
                  %em fixed point of the adjunction
                  if its
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction+unit"} adjunction unit
                  is an
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi c
                        %munderover
                          %mo ⟶
                          %mo ≃
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi η
                              %mi c
                        %mi R
                        %mi L
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi c
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        c \underoverset{\simeq}{\eta_c}{\longrightarrow} R L (c)
                %p and write
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒞
                          %mi fix
                        %mo ↪
                        %mi 𝒞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\mathcal{C}_{fix} \hookrightarrow \mathcal{C}
                %p
                  for the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
                  on these fixed objects;
              %li
                %p
                  an
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi 𝒟
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \in \mathcal{D}
                  is a
                  %em fixed point of the adjunction
                  if its
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction+counit"} adjunction counit
                  is an
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi L
                        %mi R
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi d
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %munderover
                          %mo ⟶
                          %mo ≃
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi ϵ
                              %mi d
                        %mi d
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        L R(d) \underoverset{\simeq}{\epsilon_d}{\longrightarrow} d
                %p and write
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒟
                          %mi fix
                        %mo ↪
                        %mi 𝒟
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\mathcal{D}_{fix} \hookrightarrow \mathcal{D}
                %p
                  for the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
                  on these fixed objects.
            %p
              Then the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/corestriction"}> co
              \-)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/restriction"} restrics
              to an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+equivalence"} adjoint equivalence
              on these
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategories"} full subcategories
              of
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fixed+points"} fixed points
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒟
                      %mi fix
                    %munderover
                      %mrow
                        %mphantom
                          %mi A
                        %mphantom
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mrow
                              %mo ⊥
                        %msub
                          %mo ≃
                          %mo ⊥
                        %mphantom
                          %mi A
                      %munder
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mi R
                      %mover
                        %mo ⟵
                        %mi L
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mi fix
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{D}_{fix}
                    \\underoverset
                    {\underset{ R }{\longrightarrow}}
                    {\overset{ L }{\longleftarrow}}
                    {\phantom{A}\phantom{{}_{\bot}}\simeq_{\bot}\phantom{A}}
                    \\mathcal{C}_{fix}
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              It is sufficient to see that the functors (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/corestriction"}> co-
              )
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/restriction"} restrict
              as claimed, for then the restricted adjunction unit/counit are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphisms"} isomorphisms
              by definition, and hence exhibit an
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+equivalence"} adjoint equivalence
            %p Hence we need to show that
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  for
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi c
                        %mo ∈
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒞
                          %mi fix
                        %mo ↪
                        %mi 𝒞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \in \mathcal{C}_{fix} \hookrightarrow \mathcal{C}
                  we have that
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ϵ
                          %mrow
                            %mi L
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi c
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon_{L(c)}
                  is an
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
              %li
                %p
                  for
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mo ∈
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒟
                          %mi fix
                        %mo ↪
                        %mi 𝒟
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \in \mathcal{D}_{fix} \hookrightarrow \mathcal{D}
                  we have that
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi η
                          %mrow
                            %mi R
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi d
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta_{R(d)}
                  is an
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            %p
              For the first case we claim that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi η
                      %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L(\eta_{c})
              provides an
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+morphism"} inverse
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangle+identity"} triangle identity
              it is a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+inverse"} right inverse
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invertible+morphism"} invertible morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϵ
                      %mrow
                        %mi L
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi c
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon_{L(c)}
              is an isomorphism.
            %p
              The second claim is
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+duality"} formally dual
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              Notice the above theorem and proof are valid in any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
              admitting
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverters"} inverters
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mi fix
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}_{fix}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒟
                      %mi fix
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}_{fix}
              can be presented, respectively, as the inverter of the unit and the counit of the adjunction. Then the proof only uses the unit/counit definition of adjunction, which is valid in every 2-category (see
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
          %h2#properties Properties
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The fixed point construction can be seen as a 2-adjoint. See
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/envelope+of+an+adjunction"} envelope of an adjunction
                for details.
            %li
              %p
                If the adjunction is
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+adjunction"} idempotent
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
                are precisely those of the form
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                        %mi d
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G d
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒟
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
                are those of the form
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                        %mi c
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F c
            %li
              %p
                At the other extreme, it may be the case that there are no fixed points of an adjunction, and the restriction to an equivalence is between empty categories. An example is the adjunction between sets and
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+sets"} pointed sets
          %h2#Examples Examples
          %h3#GelfandDuality Gelfand duality
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gelfand+duality"} Gelfand duality
            is (a further restriction of) the fixed point equivalence of the adjunction between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compactly+generated+topological+spaces"} compactly generated
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hausdorff+spaces"} Hausdorff spaces
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+algebras"} topological algebras
            over the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
            as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dualizing+object"} dualizing object
            (
            %a{:href => "#PorstTholen91"}> Porst-Tholen 91, section 4-c
            ).
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/envelope+of+an+adjunction"} envelope of an adjunction
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nucleus+of+a+profunctor"} nucleus of a profunctor
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+adjunction"} idempotent adjunction
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li#PorstTholen91
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hans-E.+Porst"}> Hans-E. Porst
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Walter+Tholen"} Walter Tholen
              %em Concrete Dualities
              in H. Herrlich,
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hans-E.+Porst"} Hans-E. Porst
              (eds.)
              = succeed "," do
                %em Category Theory at Work
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.heldermann.de/R&E/RAE18/ctw07.pdf"} pdf
